{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.25, 
 "rationale": "The claim is a specific Distributed‑Morphology style claim about Northern/Sámi morphosyntax for which I find no direct descriptive evidence. Northern Sámi does have stative/stative‑like morphology, but published descriptions do not report a general pattern where vocabulary items that could spell out a ‘stative verbaliser’ are systematically blocked from appearing in a purported ‘sensive verb phrase’ because insertion conditions fail. The statement mixes technical DM terminology with an unclear category ('sensive verb phrase') and is not supported by standard descriptive literature, so it is not verifiably true."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.60, 
 "rationale": "The statement is overly general. Descriptive work shows Spanish permits gapping in coordination (including coordinated clauses), but gapping is restricted and often unacceptable in non‑coordinate embedded contexts (e.g. inside arbitrary complement NPs/PPs). Therefore a blanket claim that “embedded gapping is acceptable in Spanish” is false."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.60, 
 "rationale": "There is no consensus that a single constructional/fragment-based analysis uniformly covers non-embedded and embedded gapping. Empirical differences (acceptability, island effects, prosodic/licensing patterns) lead many descriptive accounts to impose different constraints or derivations for embedded vs. non‑embedded gapping, and theoretical work is contested on whether a single construction suffices."}

{"veredict": true, 
 "confidence": 0.65, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive syntactic literature reports that Indonesian allows prepositionless wh-remnants in sluicing: prepositions that are otherwise required in full clauses can be omitted inside the ellipsis site, yielding grammatical sluiced questions (i.e. the wh remnant appears without the overt preposition). This pattern has been observed in descriptive and theoretical work on sluicing cross-linguistically for Indonesian."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.85, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive evidence shows Russian requires the preposition to remain with its wh‑complement (pied‑piping: e.g. 's kem'), and standard syntactic work reports that languages that disallow P‑stranding (like Russian) do not permit omitting the preposition under sluicing."}

{"veredict": false,
 "confidence": 0.70,
 "rationale": "Descriptive work and native-data show Modern Greek requires the preposition on the wh-remnant in sluicing (e.g. 'Μίλησε με κάποιον, αλλά δεν ξέρω από ποιον' — not *'...δεν ξέρω ποιον'). Greek does not pattern with languages that permit preposition omission under sluicing."}

{"veredict": true, 
 "confidence": 0.85, 
 "rationale": "In standard/descriptive German prepositions must pied‑pipe with their complements in wh‑questions and relative clauses (Mit wem?; mit dem), so preposition‑stranding is generally ungrammatical. Some colloquial dialects allow stranded prepositions, hence less than maximal confidence."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.85, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive work shows German requires the preposition to surface with the wh-remnant (e.g. 'mit wem', 'worüber'); sluicing in German does not generally allow omission of an antecedent preposition, so preposition-dropping under sluicing is not attested as a general property."}

{"veredict": true, 
 "confidence": 0.70, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive work on sluicing reports that omitting the preposition in the remnant is more acceptable when the correlate is a full nominal (e.g. an indefinite DP) than when it is a pronoun. The Minimize Forms (MiF) idea — that grammars prefer reduced/shorter forms when possible in ellipsis — predicts this pattern: P-omission yields a smaller remnant and is thus favored with nominal correlates, whereas pronominal correlates tend to resist P-omission. (Language-specific variation and theoretical details reduce confidence.)"}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.65, 
 "rationale": "In Northern Sámi -it is the infinitive/verb ending (inflectional) and the segment written š (/ʃ/) is a derivational/stem-forming suffix; derivational material in Sámi normally attaches to the stem before inflectional endings. In other words the expected order is stem + /ʃ/ + -it (not -it + /ʃ/), so the statement as given is incorrect."}

{
  "veredict": false,
  "confidence": 0.87,
  "rationale": "Psycholinguistic evidence (eye‑tracking, reading‑time, and ERP studies) indicates native English speakers rapidly use gender information during pronoun resolution: gender mismatches produce immediate processing effects (e.g. earliest reading‑time disruptions and ERP correlates), rather than generally 'slow' use. Ambiguous forms like singular 'they' can show different patterns, but this does not support a blanket claim of slow use of gender information."
}

{"veredict": true, 
 "confidence": 0.65, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive work on Spanish shows embedded gapping patterns that are hard to derive from a pure Large Conjunct (high/clausal) coordination account: gapping can occur across complementizer boundaries, shows extraction/island and word‑order effects incompatible with treating the conjoined material as full clauses, and is better captured by analyses positing smaller coordinated domains (e.g. remnant movement + ellipsis or VP/TP‑internal coordination). These empirical mismatches therefore argue against a straightforward Large Conjunct Gapping approach."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.45, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive work on Russian shows P-omission in sluicing is largely constrained by syntactic facts (Russian disfavors preposition-stranding and generally requires pied-piping), not by phonological length of the preposition; there is no robust descriptive evidence that longer prepositions reliably reduce P-omission in Russian."}

{"veredict": true, 
 "confidence": 0.70, 
 "rationale": "Under the descriptive MiF (Minimize Forms) idea in sluicing literature, ellipsis favors the minimally specified wh-form compatible with antecedent identity. A nominal (full NP) correlate carries more morphological/lexical content to be matched, so the wh-remnant is less likely to omit an overt preposition than when the correlate is a pronominal (which is less specified and thus allows a smaller—preposition-omitting—remnant). This prediction is standard in discussions of sluicing and P‑omission."}

{"veredict": true,
 "confidence": 0.55,
 "rationale": "Descriptive work and experimental studies report a tendency for wh-remnants to retain an antecedent preposition when the antecedent contains a fully lexical (\"contentful\") PP correlate; omitting the P in the remnant is more acceptable when the correlate is non-specific/pronominal or when English allows P-stranding. The effect is gradient and language-dependent, so the generalization holds descriptively but not exceptionlessly."}

{"veredict": true, 
 "confidence": 0.75, 
 "rationale": "Descriptively, Samoan shows split intransitivity: some intransitive (typically active/unergative or agentive) verbs pattern with transitive agents and take non‑absolutive (ergative/agent‑like) subject forms rather than absolutive marking."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.80, 
 "rationale": "Empirically and descriptively, verbs with agentive semantics that take an absolutive subject pattern as unergative (external/agentive subject), not as unaccusative (internal/patient subject). While Eastern Basque shows complex splits in ergativity and agreement, the descriptive literature does not treat agentive absolutive-subject verbs as unaccusative; unaccusativity is reserved for verbs whose single argument is underlyingly internal/patiental."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.60, 
 "rationale": "The claim is too categorical. Northern Sámi orthography uses the letter š (/ʃ/) in derivational morphology, and there are deadjectival/denominal verbalizers that yield intransitive verbs, but the presence of a /ʃ/-forming suffix does not uniformly produce intransitives. Different derivational suffixes (and the same suffix in different lexical items) can yield intransitive, transitive, or anticausative meanings, so one cannot say attachment of /ʃ/ to an adjective or noun always returns an intransitive verb."}

{"veredict": false,
 "confidence": 0.60,
 "rationale": "This claim is too strong. The origin of prepositionless remnants in sluicing is debated: some analyses do derive them from pied‑piped PPs with PF‑deletion of the preposition, but others derive them via extraction out of the PP, LF operations, or language‑specific PF constraints. Cross‑linguistic evidence varies (languages that require pied‑piping sometimes disallow prepositionless sluices), so preposition omission under sluicing is not universally derived from pied‑piping in regular wh‑questions."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.90, 
 "rationale": "Descriptively, English permits sluices that include a preposition (often in more formal registers). Examples: 'I talked to someone, but I don't know to whom' and 'She was talking with someone, but I don't know with whom.' Linguistic work on sluicing (e.g. Merchant) treats such preposition-including remnants as grammatical, though their formality and some distributional constraints vary."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.80, 
 "rationale": "In cleft-deletion accounts the sluice is derived from a cleft whose focused/XP (the cleft pivot) survives while the rest is deleted; the overt wh-remnant corresponds to that cleft pivot/focus. (There are alternative analyses of sluicing, so confidence is not maximal.)"}

{"veredict": true, 
 "confidence": 0.70, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive literature on sluicing in Romance (including Spanish) includes proposals that prepositionless wh-remnants are derived via an underlying cleft-like structure (the wh-element as cleft pivot) that does not undergo subsequent deletion; this is a documented, though contested, analysis among alternatives (e.g. remnant-movement plus TP-deletion, pied‑piping analyses)."}

{"veredict": false, 
 "confidence": 0.70, 
 "rationale": "Descriptive work on sluicing shows preposition omission is generally more acceptable with full nominal correlates than with pronominal correlates; pronouns tend to favor retaining the preposition for recoverability. MiF (Minimize Forms) in the literature is invoked to prefer the minimally different/recoverable remnant, which in practice does not predict greater P-omission with pronominal correlates."}
